 ('not', 'love.'): {'*E*': 1}}\n",
      "From fairest creatures we desire increase, That thereby beauty's rose might never die, But as the sun is daily new and old, So long as men can breathe, or eyes can see, So long as brain and heart a league is took, And each doth good turns eyes for eyes have done: Mine eyes have done: Mine eyes have done: Mine eyes have err'd, And to the sober west, As those whose beauties proudly make them born to our desire Than think that we before have heard them told. Thy registers and thee I both defy, Not wondering at the present nor the past, For thy records and what we see doth lie, Made more or less by thy granting? And for this sin there is no remedy, It is so deem'd Not by our feeling, but by others' seeing: For why should I haste me thence? Till I return, of posting is no stronger than a flower? O! how shall summer's honey breath hold out, Against the stormy gusts of winter's day And barren rage of death's eternal cold? O! none but unthrifts. Dear my love, to whom my jewels trifles are, Most worthy comfort, now my gracious numbers are decay'd, And my sick Muse doth give an other place. I grant, sweet love, thy lovely argument Deserves the travail of a worthier pen; Yet what of thee to give? Profitless usurer, why dost thou abuse The bounteous largess given thee to be vile than vile esteem'd, When not to love, yet, love knows it is best painter's art. For through the cloud thou break, To dry the rain on my frailties why are frailer spies, Which in thy abundance am suffic'd, And by and by black night beauteous, and her old face new. Lo! thus, by day my limbs, by night Giving him aid, my verse can sit, Your own glass shows you when you look in it. To me, fair friend, you never can be miss'd. That poor retention could not so stout, Nor gates of steel so strong That you yourself, being extant, well might show How far I count my gain, That she hath no name, no holy bower, But is profan'd, if not paid before. But if thou shouldst in bounty cherish: She carv'd thee for her sake: But when she saw my woeful state, Straight in her cheeks; And in his former might: So, love, be of thyself so wary As I, not for that riches where is my love to-day, to-morrow kind, Still constant in a cool well by, Which from Love's fire heats water, water cools not love.\n"
